#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119592 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119778 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b022adb-2663-550f-866d-3365bb2d78c9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a Product"}] :question/id #uuid "c5560ec9-32d6-514d-92fa-fbd77500d943" :question/math-academy-id "e-4431" :question/problem "Given that ![](images/fc/fcc4c2fb5ffaf79bd40a967fa0f3ff3ed3c25a4bc91e831f679aa2162d93050b.svg) find ![](images/3a/3a950a6005df11dc3df83b40a796c8e5ce962b591e040207bb961da532ec92dd.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Given two ![](images/b0/b0cdde792f80dc6b0c5defb2f8d767ae115ecf922fa46f2a233f10959e509870.svg) matrices ![](images/34/34058a2a5d1de088feed7d9825ba218394c9a8bb880b046be7b47307cdd37ce1.svg) and ![](images/50/504b9ae6bd4255a78e26a2d32aaca2d613171ad01c9ac5d992f5512d093605e7.svg) we have\n\n![](images/3f/3fbf04c7a536c85422f2762eb5475ff5edfcff7a71eee0fa3cc14143e79bb10f.svg)\n\nIn our case, this gives\n\n![](images/16/16c31a7e010da9d8f4e17628bc1c7b5dd3ef1d65a4e5e191bec2fd404135a7bc.svg)\n\nWe can compute the required determinants as follows:\n\n- For the first determinant, we notice that it's upper triangular. Therefore, the determinant is simply the product of the diagonal entries.\n\n- For the second determinant, we can compute it using a Laplace expansion down the ![](images/95/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg)st column.\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n![](images/27/273e8879a6f78121b98902852e974aef15d9408e7cd026d3cc17e51837248ede.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119593 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119779 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "164c56ea-afbb-5ce4-800a-19002695db76" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of the Inverse of a Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "17487f8f-4974-5aaf-8729-6b1336ca5eaa" :question/math-academy-id "e-4432" :question/problem "Find $|A^{-1}|,$ if  ![](images/8e/8e92cd7ddbecf627452e4fa63d2af25fc60fad228bf42b5c4f8c9dfad28f4971.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n|A^{-1}| = \\dfrac{1}{|A|}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $|A|$ using a Laplace expansion across the $3$rd row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A| & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 14 & -1 \\\\ 7 & -6 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-3(-1)^{3+2}|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 7 & -6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-12+7) \\\\  & =-15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\det(A^{-1}) =  \\dfrac{1}{|A|} = -\\dfrac{1}{15}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119594 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119780 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e4dd07e-475b-5c89-883b-0e4adcf6a723" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant Using the Linearity of the Determinant"}] :question/id #uuid "d5d020cb-50ee-53f6-9d8b-d384bc9b7999" :question/math-academy-id "e-4433" :question/problem "Given that \n![](images/58/589324d1b345360020e9c2b1096b64a08008a52b05a13fdc7fd39bcb063a8d58.svg)\nfind \n![](images/f4/f4925a93ca952863cac520a853c1bc5d7c815dc8324043d6c0963d20d2b2b1ff.svg)" :question/worked-solution "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}a & b & c+1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}a+0 & b+0 & c+1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants and expanding the second determinant across the $1$st row, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}a+0 & b+0 & c+1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & ={{|\\begin{aligned}a & b & c \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|}_{⏟}}_{-10}+|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-10+1(-1)^{1+3}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-10-1 \\\\  & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420854 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119778 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b022adb-2663-550f-866d-3365bb2d78c9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a0c86ff-0d80-5008-95a2-bec3a1c1a808" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420799} {:answer/id #uuid "11030227-d4ce-5b70-8894-186891176d6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420800} {:answer/id #uuid "7dd0e1b8-d837-556e-96c2-a100f45e8fe7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420816} {:answer/id #uuid "b4fdf14b-3a08-5a96-9420-377d1a8d1538" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420822} {:answer/id #uuid "daea1037-0203-5328-a357-a7d37817bb29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420833}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7dd0e1b8-d837-556e-96c2-a100f45e8fe7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420816} :answer-field/id #uuid "57142131-309b-5e63-93f0-085dd8ef09bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420839}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b1079762-fa86-56ef-a06d-de94245b17fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-130474" :question/problem "Given that $C=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 5 \\\\ -6 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],$ find $det(C).$" :question/worked-solution "Given two $n\\times n$ matrices $M$ and $N,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet(MN)=det(M)\\cdot det(N).\n$$\n\nIn our case, this gives\n\n$$\ndet(C)=det[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot det[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 5 \\\\ -6 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe can compute the required determinants as follows:\n\n- For the first determinant, we can compute it using a Laplace expansion across the $3$rd row.\n\n- For the second determinant, we can compute it using a Laplace expansion down the $2$nd column.\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(C) & =|\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 5 \\\\ -6 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(-1)^{3+3}|\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|\\cdot (-1)(-1)^{2+2}|\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(6-4)\\cdot (-1)(12-5) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-7) \\\\  & =-28.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265920 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119778 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b022adb-2663-550f-866d-3365bb2d78c9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "702b6328-c32f-5527-b2ca-d3baaadb31ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263970} {:answer/id #uuid "7d75795f-2f71-5a1a-b8cc-d1f72992101b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-214" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264081} {:answer/id #uuid "9318164d-2060-5f48-886c-878e646e7c79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-216" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264260} {:answer/id #uuid "ab0a2ee8-8586-50ff-ab69-638797faa787" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "206" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264468} {:answer/id #uuid "f2a8cc04-1d7d-5562-8be8-65ee50e1bcc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "126" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265132}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9318164d-2060-5f48-886c-878e646e7c79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-216" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264260} :answer-field/id #uuid "14d64a4d-a6ea-5c2c-a215-d5f4325e92b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265277}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6a11bbff-ca26-5c49-a8ab-b5946f4034f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-130493" :question/problem "Given that $A=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & 7 \\\\ 4 & 8\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 2\\end{aligned}],$ find $det(A).$" :question/worked-solution "Given two $n\\times n$ matrices $M$ and $N,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet(MN)=det(M)\\cdot det(N).\n$$\n\nIn our case, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & 7 \\\\ 4 & 8\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot det[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(40-28)\\cdot (6-24) \\\\  & =12\\cdot (-18) \\\\  & =-216.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265928 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119779 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "164c56ea-afbb-5ce4-800a-19002695db76" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of the Inverse of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14df6deb-d3ee-5e1a-ad59-ff7cc32a5d43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{38}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263194} {:answer/id #uuid "5cad807e-0569-5ff3-9e40-730b8b912410" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263801} {:answer/id #uuid "79d24e48-d809-52e3-ad2f-fd1e75e9882a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{28}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264056} {:answer/id #uuid "aac71a88-0dd8-5c6f-afdc-8df09b432ac7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264462} {:answer/id #uuid "ed152b32-61af-5f9e-b9dd-61f70984cef8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265069}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14df6deb-d3ee-5e1a-ad59-ff7cc32a5d43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{38}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263194} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f7c60c4-fc52-503f-8ef4-67b53122d11d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265365}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "42eb872e-48fc-5f57-9397-6ecc0f021c29" :question/math-academy-id "q-130529" :question/problem "Find $|{A}^{-1}|,$ if $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n|{A}^{-1}|=\\frac{1}{|A|}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $|A|$ using a Laplace expansion across the $2$nd column:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A| & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(-1)^{1+2}|\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|+1(-1)^{2+2}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|+(-1)(-1)^{3+2}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2(-12+2)+(9-5)+(-6+20) \\\\  & =20+4+14 \\\\  & =38\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n|{A}^{-1}|=\\frac{1}{|A|}=\\frac{1}{38}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420852 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119780 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e4dd07e-475b-5c89-883b-0e4adcf6a723" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant Using the Linearity of the Determinant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f66b246-867d-5382-b745-224f4b0b1960" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-276" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420805} {:answer/id #uuid "6f34b981-25b3-5127-9dcc-d184355dec85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "429" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420814} {:answer/id #uuid "bbaccfd8-0fee-5b02-b497-78e555afcf98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "294" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420826} {:answer/id #uuid "dd980cfa-ae7f-54ba-bba4-12f039277176" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-372" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420835} {:answer/id #uuid "e9605807-1150-5819-807b-86c3f17e6ec2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-346" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420838}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd980cfa-ae7f-54ba-bba4-12f039277176" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-372" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420835} :answer-field/id #uuid "96ea088a-9b87-51d6-9259-b1c59aec445a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420843}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8c3dc4f1-ad56-595b-a7eb-41a85271c7fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-130693" :question/problem "Find\n$|\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 114 & 210 & 198 \\\\ 113 & 210 & 198\\end{aligned}|=$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the $2$nd row of our determinant is almost equal to the $3$rd row. So, we can rewrite the given determinant, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 114 & 210 & 198 \\\\ 113 & 210 & 198\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 113+1 & 210+0 & 198+0 \\\\ 113 & 210 & 198\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, using the linearity property of determinants, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 113+1 & 210+0 & 198+0 \\\\ 113 & 210 & 198\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 113 & 210 & 198 \\\\ 113 & 210 & 198\\end{aligned}|+|\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 113 & 210 & 198\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first determinant must be zero since it has two identical rows, while the second determinant can be expanded across the $2$nd row, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 113 & 210 & 198 \\\\ 113 & 210 & 198\\end{aligned}|+|\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 113 & 210 & 198\\end{aligned}| & =0+1(-1)^{2+1}|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 210 & 198\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(4\\cdot 198-2\\cdot 210) \\\\  & =-(792-420) \\\\  & =-372\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265974 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119780 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e4dd07e-475b-5c89-883b-0e4adcf6a723" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant Using the Linearity of the Determinant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "331f3993-b5ac-54a2-ac82-06dd0321b151" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263442} {:answer/id #uuid "3603ab83-8aae-5dd5-89fa-afe1ef9f62d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263477} {:answer/id #uuid "57d3925a-c1e7-542d-8f33-1d3909394cd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263757} {:answer/id #uuid "c0ead8f3-49e7-5a32-8b04-bc4291058f3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264675} {:answer/id #uuid "c9e9d9c7-1fa1-50bc-b6dc-efdafdea5ab7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264765}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3603ab83-8aae-5dd5-89fa-afe1ef9f62d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263477} :answer-field/id #uuid "6691a242-c5c1-5177-b03a-f829842b5ebb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265425}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "74270614-19f5-5cbe-a862-b299585b014f" :question/math-academy-id "q-130777" :question/problem "Given that \n$|\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ a & b+2\\end{aligned}|=8$,\nfind \n$|\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ a+3 & b\\end{aligned}|.$" :question/worked-solution "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ a+3 & b\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ a+3 & (b+2)-2\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ a+3 & (b+2)-2\\end{aligned}| & ={{|\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ a & b+2\\end{aligned}|}_{⏟}}_{8}+|\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =8+(-6+6) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420850 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119778 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b022adb-2663-550f-866d-3365bb2d78c9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1de3d101-6ebd-5bbc-ad0b-5f1d9ed17bbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420803} {:answer/id #uuid "3896dbb3-7bb1-5f7c-b8bf-88d4cf58a131" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420808} {:answer/id #uuid "d2f0dcd3-6430-515f-8e85-02e0bf1c5fa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420832} {:answer/id #uuid "dd787881-42cf-53fe-9c3e-08b97bcc5ccf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420834} {:answer/id #uuid "e50a27dc-031b-52dd-8815-f79824db9e9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420837}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1de3d101-6ebd-5bbc-ad0b-5f1d9ed17bbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420803} :answer-field/id #uuid "db7de380-466f-5089-9f2e-e91927307109" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420846}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7f0a84be-936b-55f9-b169-54a768974c12" :question/math-academy-id "q-42577" :question/problem "Given that $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -7 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],$ find $det(A).$" :question/worked-solution "Given two $n\\times n$ matrices $M$ and $N,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet(MN)=det(M)\\cdot det(N).\n$$\n\nIn our case, this gives\n\n$$\ndet(A)=det[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot det[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -7 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe can compute the required determinants as follows:\n\n- For the first determinant, we can compute it using a Laplace expansion across the $3$rd row.\n\n- For the second determinant, we can compute it using a Laplace expansion down the $2$nd column.\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -7 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(-1)^{3+3}|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}|\\cdot 1(-1)^{2+2}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(2-1)\\cdot (8-6) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420847 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119779 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "164c56ea-afbb-5ce4-800a-19002695db76" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of the Inverse of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "336cb96e-8d35-5f9f-92e4-bf01f1fe608d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420806} {:answer/id #uuid "5725181a-ff3e-5107-8722-cd79c0a511e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420811} {:answer/id #uuid "b75e75d9-4872-5fbc-a59d-3e54eebab508" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420824} {:answer/id #uuid "bb525c0d-2a19-503f-b30d-3b12439ddf16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420825} {:answer/id #uuid "cab0cf74-f931-5eb5-9974-e3db30298fa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420829}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb525c0d-2a19-503f-b30d-3b12439ddf16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420825} :answer-field/id #uuid "75968549-80b9-5200-918a-66a961c78ce2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420842}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0b58d794-d6a1-55fe-88d5-660b59bdd30a" :question/math-academy-id "q-42578" :question/problem "Find $det({A}^{-1}),$ if $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 5\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet({A}^{-1})=\\frac{1}{det(A)}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $det(A):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =20-18 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\ndet({A}^{-1})=\\frac{1}{det(A)}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120436 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119779 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "164c56ea-afbb-5ce4-800a-19002695db76" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of the Inverse of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b298ccb-6d42-51e0-bec7-bdcce97b859a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327030} {:answer/id #uuid "104af8d6-6df5-59fb-8200-c18490b4fd4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{37}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327079} {:answer/id #uuid "2d8e5b8a-f047-5394-af88-102417a6f0e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{45}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327338} {:answer/id #uuid "3fde6c7e-e80c-5ec0-964a-81bf5acc2144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{75}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327492} {:answer/id #uuid "c150ce04-23ed-5152-a4c5-802328f796f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328621}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b298ccb-6d42-51e0-bec7-bdcce97b859a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327030} :answer-field/id #uuid "7775f4a0-2beb-5ee4-8f08-e48b58e45535" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329383}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e713fddd-f608-51f9-ac5a-f16a9c918c04" :question/math-academy-id "q-42584" :question/problem "Find $|{A}^{-1}|,$ if $A=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -6 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n|{A}^{-1}|=\\frac{1}{|A|}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $|A|$ using a Laplace expansion across the $2$nd column:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A| & =|\\begin{aligned}-5 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -6 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1(-1)^{1+2}|\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{aligned}|+1(-1)^{2+2}|\\begin{aligned}-5 & 4 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{aligned}|-2(-1)^{3+2}|\\begin{aligned}-5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(12-12)+(-15+24)+2(10-16) \\\\  & =0+9-12 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n|{A}^{-1}|=\\frac{1}{|A|}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120449 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119778 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b022adb-2663-550f-866d-3365bb2d78c9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "884c1925-0ccc-54e5-8235-15f4852ab5f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118809} {:answer/id #uuid "974ae98b-f65f-5d56-abb2-235fcf5d1709" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118810} {:answer/id #uuid "a0f4f7fc-49f1-51bb-a52f-e78439376e11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118811} {:answer/id #uuid "a7c1329a-446a-56c8-94dc-f3d8fb69e271" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "98" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118812} {:answer/id #uuid "665f03b3-8fe1-5194-816a-e67e96a71c55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118813}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "884c1925-0ccc-54e5-8235-15f4852ab5f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118809} :answer-field/id #uuid "56464aea-61e2-5f04-a701-b6c9323c4b82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118808}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "daa9792e-2290-5f44-bdfd-5cde9ef8123b" :question/math-academy-id "q-42996" :question/problem "Given that $A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}],$ find $det(A).$" :question/worked-solution "Given two $n\\times n$ matrices $M$ and $N,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet(MN)=det(M)\\cdot det(N).\n$$\n\nIn our case, this gives\n\n$$\ndet(A)=det[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot det[\\begin{aligned}3 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that both matrices are triangular. Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =(5\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 2)\\cdot (3\\cdot 2\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =(-10)\\cdot (-12) \\\\  & =120.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420853 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119779 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "164c56ea-afbb-5ce4-800a-19002695db76" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of the Inverse of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15bb6772-ebfa-5300-9707-f0c44ba3ca38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420801} {:answer/id #uuid "1657176b-b7bb-5bac-8681-50357708185d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420802} {:answer/id #uuid "341db512-89dd-5d60-9521-449fcc83f4d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420807} {:answer/id #uuid "7ecdfc0d-790a-509f-8041-8c18f54a719a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420817} {:answer/id #uuid "bd130b49-aec7-5947-9ffc-20dcb30a8a97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420827}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd130b49-aec7-5947-9ffc-20dcb30a8a97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420827} :answer-field/id #uuid "a81cf8c4-10bc-54bc-9308-65fe3b30e2ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420844}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9e9ddda3-97aa-551d-9453-cae76713ec59" :question/math-academy-id "q-43070" :question/problem "Find $det({A}^{-1}),$ if $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 5\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet({A}^{-1})=\\frac{1}{det(A)}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $det(A):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =10-5 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\ndet({A}^{-1})=\\frac{1}{det(A)}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420848 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119779 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "164c56ea-afbb-5ce4-800a-19002695db76" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of the Inverse of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3dafe486-15fc-5abc-8a0d-2beb22386f42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420809} {:answer/id #uuid "8e814c6a-3786-561d-9f46-38ef12a0585a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420818} {:answer/id #uuid "90c1ce8c-7b18-5377-9a47-ace432f229bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420819} {:answer/id #uuid "cc7cb828-c853-54d8-b205-d802c97b37f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420830} {:answer/id #uuid "d2b62576-113c-5f4d-b29d-3ce30a3f8585" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420831}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90c1ce8c-7b18-5377-9a47-ace432f229bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420819} :answer-field/id #uuid "660640e3-e8ef-509a-a758-3c65a1428537" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420841}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "37f105cd-9dd0-500b-bb29-7f9f80a1a14c" :question/math-academy-id "q-43071" :question/problem "Find $det({A}^{-1}),$ if $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet({A}^{-1})=\\frac{1}{det(A)}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $det(A):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =6-2 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\ndet({A}^{-1})=\\frac{1}{det(A)}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120457 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119780 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e4dd07e-475b-5c89-883b-0e4adcf6a723" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant Using the Linearity of the Determinant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01e097d8-e542-5acf-a330-fe6eeb6f27a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,146" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326952} {:answer/id #uuid "138116bf-b723-50e7-9f4c-0360ed73a988" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-434" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327101} {:answer/id #uuid "a0898f35-ae35-5a2c-85c3-2c1973fb0a46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "870" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328324} {:answer/id #uuid "a7d333b3-316e-527a-9482-d8e7753af3e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "440" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328390} {:answer/id #uuid "c8b3cbac-6be7-58e9-bfd2-408356f2bda6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-424" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328699}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "138116bf-b723-50e7-9f4c-0360ed73a988" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-434" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327101} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a480665-7d5c-59ec-8577-d65fbb82b058" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329200}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6ac6b8ae-6cc0-5802-bd63-e9b3982c4a3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-43097" :question/problem "$|\\begin{aligned}107 & 215 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 11 \\\\ 107 & 215 & 100\\end{aligned}|=$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the $1$st row of our determinant is almost equal to the $3$rd row. So, we can rewrite the given determinant, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}107 & 215 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 11 \\\\ 107 & 215 & 100\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}107 & 215 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 11 \\\\ 107+0 & 215+0 & 99+1\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, using the linearity property of determinants, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}107 & 215 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 11 \\\\ 107+0 & 215+0 & 99+1\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}107 & 215 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 11 \\\\ 107 & 215 & 99\\end{aligned}|+|\\begin{aligned}107 & 215 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first determinant must be zero since it has two identical rows, while the second determinant can be expanded across the $3$rd row, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}107 & 215 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 11 \\\\ 107 & 215 & 99\\end{aligned}|+|\\begin{aligned}107 & 215 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =0+1(-1)^{3+3}|\\begin{aligned}107 & 215 \\\\ 6 & 8\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =107\\cdot 8-215\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =-434\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420849 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119778 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b022adb-2663-550f-866d-3365bb2d78c9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44a67a7f-7ce3-5fc4-9a99-c7b43778cf51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "77" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420810} {:answer/id #uuid "755ff62c-e4b0-56e4-8933-fdd296936d8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420815} {:answer/id #uuid "9577d942-a37d-5bb6-ad63-4cff94938a48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420820} {:answer/id #uuid "b74bd5d3-d00b-534f-908c-090b06c3201d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420823} {:answer/id #uuid "c498389f-d133-538a-bef9-88e693ebc92c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "76" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420828}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "755ff62c-e4b0-56e4-8933-fdd296936d8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420815} :answer-field/id #uuid "633fd2f4-984e-51bc-b51a-5f37a89e06b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420840}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "38d108f6-377b-5951-bc6b-4ec43d8b652b" :question/math-academy-id "q-88281" :question/problem "Given that $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}],$ find $det(A).$" :question/worked-solution "Given two $n\\times n$ matrices $M$ and $N,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet(MN)=det(M)\\cdot det(N).\n$$\n\nIn our case, this gives\n\n$$\ndet(A)=det[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot det[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that both matrices are triangular. Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =(3\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 2)\\cdot ((-1)\\cdot 3\\cdot (-4)) \\\\  & =(-6)\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =-72.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354636 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119780 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e4dd07e-475b-5c89-883b-0e4adcf6a723" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant Using the Linearity of the Determinant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47596227-58dd-5c85-b31b-dd2085e9d721" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352226} {:answer/id #uuid "555a8298-192e-50be-8d44-14723e16f11d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352323} {:answer/id #uuid "58619be6-c805-5344-9021-173a95976dd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352344} {:answer/id #uuid "bf9db913-3c22-5c37-be9e-ca617e5d0dee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353213} {:answer/id #uuid "e7c2b5b7-b8a6-51db-98b0-9f4ba4bb301f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353585}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47596227-58dd-5c85-b31b-dd2085e9d721" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352226} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bd53aab-78d5-531e-a248-9e6ce0d0938b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353889}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "31688145-09ed-5774-a522-d76e627c7dee" :question/math-academy-id "q-88296" :question/problem "Given that \n$|\\begin{aligned}a & b & c \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|=4,$\nfind \n$|\\begin{aligned}a+1 & b & c \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|.$" :question/worked-solution "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}a+1 & b & c \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}a+1 & b+0 & c+0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants and expanding the second determinant across the $1$st row, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}a+1 & b+0 & c+0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & ={{|\\begin{aligned}a & b & c \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|}_{⏟}}_{4}+|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4+1(-1)^{1+1}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4+1 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420851 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119780 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e4dd07e-475b-5c89-883b-0e4adcf6a723" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant Using the Linearity of the Determinant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "256655f5-3716-5c72-b038-769fa376cf93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420804} {:answer/id #uuid "65294e4c-a8db-5c12-befc-326f1199fa33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420812} {:answer/id #uuid "6c92f4c3-474c-5da4-bd3e-a7ddf9353c60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,700" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420813} {:answer/id #uuid "b440a564-08a1-5c84-a502-ec561384a59f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420821} {:answer/id #uuid "e3f48478-6c84-5d48-9002-c26925eb220f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420836}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "256655f5-3716-5c72-b038-769fa376cf93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420804} :answer-field/id #uuid "db1d1fba-e4ba-50df-bd35-d24e24ca86ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420845}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "80589b9c-bfb0-57bc-8e70-de307d877343" :question/math-academy-id "q-88306" :question/problem "$|\\begin{aligned}300 & 250 & 100 \\\\ 300 & 270 & 100 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|=$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the $1$st row of our determinant is almost equal to the $2$nd row. So, we can rewrite the given determinant, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}300 & 250 & 100 \\\\ 300 & 270 & 100 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}300 & 250 & 100 \\\\ 300+0 & 250+20 & 100+0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, using the linearity property of determinants, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}300 & 250 & 100 \\\\ 300+0 & 250+20 & 100+0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}300 & 250 & 100 \\\\ 300 & 250 & 100 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|+|\\begin{aligned}300 & 250 & 100 \\\\ 0 & 20 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first determinant must be zero since it has two identical rows, while the second determinant can be expanded across the $2$nd row, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}300 & 250 & 100 \\\\ 300 & 250 & 100 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|+|\\begin{aligned}300 & 250 & 100 \\\\ 0 & 20 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =0+20(-1)^{2+2}|\\begin{aligned}300 & 100 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =20\\cdot (300\\cdot 1-100\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =20\\cdot 200 \\\\  & =4\\,000\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
